Classify a reset or stop of a terminal or unknown stream by the ID's state.

Applies to picoquic 70b5fd5aafdd2d6c349ff783202ece9d778bcaf2, after 0001 to
0007.

0003 supplies the direction checks and refuses a stop before its context
changes. Upstream PR2279 already classifies absent retired reset IDs; that
replacement is no longer supplied here. This retains range checks, the
STOP classification and the complete contract declaration. Stream IDs are
never reused (RFC 9000 Section 2.1), and picoquic keeps each type's creation
boundary:

- An ID outside the 62-bit range answers PICOQUIC_ERROR_INVALID_STREAM_ID.
- An absent ID below its type's boundary answers
  PICOQUIC_ERROR_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED: that part is terminal and no frame is
  sent (Section 3.3). Any other absent ID stays
  PICOQUIC_ERROR_INVALID_STREAM_ID.
- A stop of a live stream whose receiving part is terminal -- reset by the
  peer, or read up to its final size (Section 3.2) -- answers
  PICOQUIC_ERROR_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED before the stream or its context
  changes.

Nothing is created, allocated or queued on any refusal.
PICOQUIC_TERMINAL_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED advertises the contract in
picoquic.h.
Tests: adapters/picoquic/tests/test_pq_stop_release.c (reset and stop
contract rows) and test_pq_hold_input.c (call order, bounded).

